# Google Earth Engine Cloud project id, registered at
# https://code.earthengine.google.com. Required by newer GEE accounts;
# leave blank to fall back to ee.Initialize() with no project (works for
# some legacy accounts). See src/auth.py.
EE_PROJECT_ID=

# Optional -- only needed for a headless server deploy (no browser to run
# `earthengine authenticate` against, e.g. a Render/Fly/Railway web
# service). Leave blank for normal local dev (interactive
# `earthengine authenticate` is used instead). When set, paste the full
# contents of a GCP service account's JSON key file (same Cloud project
# as EE_PROJECT_ID) -- see src/auth.py's module docstring for how to
# create one. Takes priority over the interactive flow when present.
GEE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_KEY_JSON=

# Maximum time for one Earth Engine Cloud API request. This bounds
# scheduler shutdown when an upstream request stops responding. Direct
# thumbnail and raster byte downloads have their own 30 s and 300 s
# transfer timeouts. Blank uses 120 seconds.
GEE_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=

# Optional -- how many days back a reading may come from before the
# system reports no imagery instead. Every satellite reading in this
# project (regional scan, /detect, the scheduler's site cache, and the
# true-color / NDCI thumbnails beside them) takes the newest usable scene
# acquired on or before the requested day, and never one acquired after
# it; this only decides when that backward search gives up. Blank uses
# sentinel2.py's DEFAULT_MAX_SCENE_AGE_DAYS (14). Raising it buys
# coverage through the monsoon at the cost of older readings -- a reading
# is only ever as informative as its own scene date, which the API and
# the map both show. At 14 days, expect most of the grid to report no
# imagery during the southwest monsoon: on the 2026-09-06 scan only 74 of
# 501 points had a usable scene that recent.
SENTINEL2_MAX_SCENE_AGE_DAYS=

# Optional -- latest-scene policy (src/scene_assess.py). Each new
# Sentinel-2 acquisition date is assessed over the WATER part of the study
# polygon (all tiles of that UTC date combined, production cloud classes
# and water mask, at the raster's 200 m scale). It passes, and may refresh
# the raster and the sites, only if it observed at least
# RASTER_SCENE_MIN_WATER_PIXELS water pixels and their cloud-flagged share
# is at most RASTER_SCENE_MAX_CLOUD_FRACTION (equality passes).
# Cloud fraction: a number in (0, 1]; blank uses sentinel2.py's
# DEFAULT_MAX_CLOUD_FRACTION (0.5), the same threshold every point reading
# uses. Minimum water pixels: a positive integer; blank uses 1000 (about
# 40 km2 at 200 m), so a scene that only grazes the polygon cannot pass.
# Read at call time; an invalid value raises an error naming the variable
# instead of falling back to the default.
RASTER_SCENE_MAX_CLOUD_FRACTION=
RASTER_SCENE_MIN_WATER_PIXELS=
